I’ll first inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen powder-XRD workflow, then build a candidate phase inventory before touching refinement. I’ll keep phase identification and nuisance/profile modeling separate, and verify the final exports against the saved GSAS-II project.

The chemistry is Ni–W–O at 900 °C, so NiWO₄ is the obvious principal candidate, but the unknown inventory could also include residual NiO/WO₃ or a less obvious Ni–W–O phase. I’m now checking the actual peak families and the supplied structure-library/index interfaces before accepting any candidate.

The observed families separate cleanly into a wolframite-type NiWO₄ pattern (including the dominant 30.87° line and matching low/mid/high-angle families) plus a monoclinic WO₃ triplet near 23–24.3° and its companion lines near 33–34°. NiO is on the watch list, but its strongest 43.4° family is absent, so the isolated 37.2° feature is not enough to include it. Next I’m comparing several physical wolframite parents and WO₃ polymorphs under a common profile model.

The first common-model refinement is running longer than a quick screening fit because GSAS-II is regenerating full dual-wavelength profiles across all 8,282 bins. The starting cells already align the dominant families to about 0.1° before angular correction; I’m checking convergence and parameter bounds before launching the candidate matrix.

The inventory challenge is decisive: orthorhombic WO₃ leaves coherent unmatched families (Rwp ≈ 9.08%), while monoclinic/triclinic models reach ≈ 6.42–6.45% under the identical fixed-coordinate nuisance model. The physically cleaner P2₁/c neutron model has much better WO₃ structure-factor residuals than the lower-symmetry P‑1 alternative, so both remain in comparison while I test wolframite parent sensitivity and then atomic-coordinate refinement.

Atomic refinement exposed the key model-quality issue: freely refining all WO₃ oxygen coordinates lowers Rwp slightly but creates chemically impossible W–O contacts (down to 0.59 Å for P‑1 and 1.30 Å for P2₁/c). I’m rejecting those overfit variants. The defensible route is refined heavy-atom positions for WO₃, with oxygen refinement confined to the simpler wolframite phase; profile broadening is now being added phase-by-phase to bring the common X term back inside the physically supplied range.

The low-angle discrepancy is background curvature, not roughness: physical roughness values did not improve the 10–20° residual and instead traded against the dominant phase scale. I’m pruning roughness and increasing only the smooth background order enough to represent the measured exponential-like decay; this should remove the broad residual without distorting phase intensities.

The revised baseline is now physically adequate across isolated low-, mid-, and high-angle families, with a positive smooth background and no bound broadening terms. NiO refines to effectively zero scale, including at its unique 43.4° line, so it is rejected. I’m performing the final symmetry-distinct texture challenge one phase at a time; only a replay-stable improvement supported across several families will be retained.

The phase inventory is stable at NiWO₄ + WO₃; NiO has refined to zero and is excluded. I’m resolving the last correlated intensity term (per-phase displacement parameters versus polarization), then I’ll freeze the defensible model, replay the saved GSAS-II project, and export all requested files from that exact project.

Fixing polarization at its independently refined value removes the apparent ADP inflation: the constrained isotropic displacement parameters settle at 0.0053 Å² (NiWO₄) and 0.0068 Å² (WO₃), both physically ordinary. The fit gain is negligible, so I’m checking their uncertainties and will retain only terms that remain independently supported.

The coordinate refinement is now at the practical limit: the WO₃ heavy-atom shifts are supported collectively, but two common-coordinate modes remain ~99% correlated. I’m preserving the best converged, physically screened state rather than chasing a numerically drifting last decimal; this uncertainty will be stated explicitly. Next is a fresh no-refinement forward replay of that frozen project.

The frozen project replayed successfully with no profile warnings and no parameter optimization. Its independently regenerated full-range fit is Rwp = 4.2288%, with 76.113 wt% NiWO₄ and 23.887 wt% WO₃; calculated intensity and background stay positive everywhere. I’m now running export-consistency and geometry checks on the submitted CIFs, then I’ll write the concise evidence/uncertainty report.

Identified two crystalline phases:

- NiWO₄ (wolframite, P 2/c): **76.11 wt%**
- WO₃ (monoclinic, P 2₁/c): **23.89 wt%**

No detectable crystalline NiO was supported. Fresh GSAS-II replay gave Rwp = **4.2288%**, Rp = **3.2519%**, with no profile or geometry warnings. The main uncertainty is the detailed WO₃ distortion; fractions carry an estimated systematic uncertainty of about ±1 wt%.

